/**
\page kcl_cemrgapp_atrialstrainmotion The CemrgAtrialStrainMotion

\imageMacro{icon.png,"Icon of CemrgAtrialStrainMotion",2.00}

\tableofcontents

\section kcl_cemrgapp_atrialstrainmotionOverview Overview

This plugin measures the regional strain of the left atrium from cine CT.

\warning Do not use this plugin for the diagnosis or the treatment of patients. It is research
software, and it comes with no warranty and no support.

The plugin reads a time series of cardiac CT frames. It builds a labelled surface mesh of the left
atrium, maps the fibre directions onto that mesh, and then tracks the mesh through the cardiac
cycle. The result is one strain value for each cell of the mesh at each time point.

The plugin gives two measures of strain:

<ul>
<li>Area strain - the change in the area of each cell of the mesh.
<li>Fibre strain - the change along the local fibre direction of each cell.
</ul>

\section kcl_cemrgapp_atrialstrainmotionPrereq Before you start

The plugin works inside one project directory. Select that directory when the plugin asks for it.
The path must contain no spaces.

Put the cine CT frames in a folder named \c nifti inside the project directory. Name the frames
\c dcm-0.nii, \c dcm-1.nii and so on. The plugin does not create this folder, and it does not
change the frames you supply.

The plugin also needs these external tools:

<ul>
<li>Docker. The plugin pulls the \c cemrg/ccta, \c cemrg/uac, \c cemrg/afmotion and openCARP images
on first use.
<li>The MIRTK binaries, in an \c MLib folder beside the CemrgApp executable.
<li>The PETSc option files, in a \c petsc_opts folder beside the CemrgApp executable.
</ul>

\section kcl_cemrgapp_atrialstrainmotionUsage The pipeline

The panel holds twenty buttons. Press them in order. Two steps are optional, and the panel shows
them in blue.

The first twelve buttons build the model:

<ul>
<li>Steps 1 and 2 segment the left atrium and extract its surface.
<li>Steps 3 to 7 identify the pulmonary veins and clip them.
<li>Step 8 improves the mesh and converts it to the CARP format.
<li>Steps 9 to 11 select the landmarks and calculate the Universal Atrial Coordinates.
<li>Step 12 maps the fibres onto the mesh and aligns the mesh to the image.
</ul>

Steps 3, 5 and 6 open the Atrial Fibres Clipper view. Follow the instructions in that view, then
return to this panel.

The last eight buttons measure the motion:

<ul>
<li>Steps 13 and 14 crop each frame and register every frame to the reference frame.
<li>Step 15 warps the mesh to each frame of the cardiac cycle.
<li>Steps 16 to 20 calculate the area strains and the fibre strains, then plot them.
</ul>

The \c Reset button clears the memory the plugin holds of the project. It changes no file on disk.

\section kcl_cemrgapp_atrialstrainmotionResults The results

The plugin writes the model into a \c UAC_CT folder, and the strain results and the plots into a
\c tracking folder. Both folders sit inside the project directory.

The plugin also writes a log into the project directory. The name of that file starts with
\c afib_log. Read the log when a step reports a failure. It holds the command that the step ran.

*/
